<http://data.silknow.org/object/0e629ef6-0717-3f19-ab62-d40890fdd4b2/observation/1>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/technical-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Long woven shawl with a black ground. The warp, dyed black for the plain black centre and inner gallery of pattern, is pinky-red for the side and end guard-stripe and main border and harlequin for the ends which are woven with weft in dark red, yellow, pale blue, light red, green, orange and dark red in areas 8-9 inches x 0.75 inch. There is a green silk warp faced twill selvedge.\n\nThe pattern of both main border (21 inches deep) and gallery (13 inches deep) is a slanted crossed cones over ogees of strapwork and semi-naturalistic flowers, the latter breaking out in cresting into the field. Guard-stripes 4.25 inches wide run round the sides and ends and separate the border from the gallery.\n\nColours, typical of the mid 1840s, are in two tones of red, a yellow that looks orange and a greenish-brown that looks a strong yellow-green, blue, pink and white. Weft is all wool, and warp is wool and silk."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/0e629ef6-0717-3f19-ab62-d40890fdd4b2> .

